  "text": "The task is to build a function `remove_adjacent_duplicates` that takes a list of integers and returns a new list with all consecutive duplicates removed. Consecutive elements that are the same must be reduced to a single occurrence, but elements that are not adjacent are preserved.\n\nTo do this, you can use a loop to go through the elements of the list while checking if the current element is the same as the previous one. If they are the same and you aren't at the beginning of the list, you skip adding this element to the new list. Otherwise, you append it to the new list. \n\nLet's implement this function properly and test our implementation.\n\n Below is the corrected code:\n\n```python\nimport unittest\n\ndef remove_adjacent_duplicates(lst):\n    if not lst:\n        return []\n    result = [lst[0]]\n    for i in range(1, len(lst)):\n        if lst[i] != result[-1]:\n            result.append(lst[i])\n    return result\n\nclass TestRemoveAdjacentDuplicates(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_basic_functionality(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4]), [1, 2, 3, 4])\n    def test_repeated_elements(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([5, 5, 5, 6, 7, 7]), [5, 6, 7])\n    def test_multiple_duplicates(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 3, 4, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5]), [1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    unittest.main()\n```\n\nIf you run this code, it will test the function correctly for all the cases.",
